          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-f19e9e1f744ddbdb\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"“Je vous aime” is a deliberate written avowal presenting Cyrano’s love as true. The phrase itself does not undertake a commitment or obligation, and the surrounding courtship situation alone does not establish performative force. Its careful prior composition and present transcription strongly oppose a reflex-like expressive reading.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The words are both written into the letter and audibly murmured, but the murmuring revoices the written avowal rather than constituting a distinct reflexive outburst.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Cyrano writes “Je vous aime” in a love letter while murmuring the words aloud as he writes; the classified event is primarily the letter’s written avowal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Cyrano is too afraid to voice his feelings directly and plans to deliver a love letter instead. He describes the letter as having been composed and recomposed internally a hundred times and as now merely being copied, making the utterance highly deliberate despite his nervous emotional state. Its intended delivery makes it a consequential act of courtship, but the core words remain an avowal rather than an explicit relational undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“CYRANO (écrivant, à part,--on l'entend murmurer de temps en temps): Je vous aime...”\",\"supports\":\"The stage direction establishes that the words are being written and intermittently murmured; their semantic form directly avows love.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cyrano calls it “Cette lettre d'amour qu'en moi-même j'ai faite / Et refaite cent fois” (“this love letter” made and remade within himself a hundred times).\",\"supports\":\"The utterance belongs to a deliberately prepared love letter, strongly supporting truth-conditional avowal and weighing against expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je n'ai tout simplement qu'à la recopier” (“All I have to do is simply copy it out”).\",\"supports\":\"The wording is presented as precomposed transcription rather than speech produced reflexively by a sudden affective state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Pas besoin de signer. Je la donne moi-même.” (“No need to sign it. I shall give it to her myself.”)\",\"supports\":\"The letter is intended as a personal declaration to its addressee, while providing no independent evidence that the core phrase undertakes a commitment or obligation.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T adequately captures the core force as a deliberate avowal. Nervousness, secrecy, written mediation, and courtship framing are contextual features and create no residual pressure beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
